6. Residual, recorded and graded, none blocking
-------------------------------------------------
- `opus.red_content` still advises on W-022 itself: "red(s) for behaviour(s)
  3 are module-load failures, not assertion-level". The red is genuinely
  assertion-level; the rule tests the whole log text (evidence.ts:213) and
  behaviour 3's assertion is *named* "ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND detected", so the
  regex matches the name. Round 2 identified this self-reference and graded
  it non-blocking; I reach the same grade independently. It cannot be
  cleared by re-recording, and neither renaming the assertion nor narrowing
  the rule is in this brief. Accepted noise — but the claim that this
  advisory is gone (builder's report) is simply false, and the record should
  not carry it.
- MODULE_LOAD_RE's `.` without the `s` flag still misses a SyntaxError
  wrapped across lines. Round 2's finding, unchanged, non-blocking, outside
  the brief.
- isDuplicateRed is byte-equality after header strip, so it catches copied
  files only. Matches the brief as written; not a defect against spec.

One condition attached to this pass (bookkeeping, not code)
-------------------------------------------------------------
W-022's tests/lint/types certificates name tree:0a333b8b, which is the
source tree at the *parent* commit 5d0c4a4. The cleanup commit added
scripts/sweep-traditio-stage.mjs — its only non-studio file — which moved
the tip's source tree to 9b996052, so `check` reports all three as
probatio.certifies.stale. This is not a W-022 defect: `git diff --stat
5d0c4a4 1fadfb6 -- packages/` is empty, so the certified tree and the tip
carry byte-identical W-022 code, and I re-ran all three gates green at the
tip myself, which is fresher evidence than the certificate. The verdict
rests on that, not on the stale certificate.

Before `done W-022`, re-cut them on the committed tip:
    bisellium verify W-022 --studio studio --repo .
(my review logs live under studio/, which verify's dirty check excludes, so
they do not obstruct it). `done` will not enforce this — stale certificates
are advisory — so it is on the orchestrator, and it is also finding F1 of
ci/cascade-23-cleanup-review.log, where the cause belongs.

Disposition
-----------
Passed. Both of round 2's blocking findings are cleared and were verified
by reproduction, not by reading the builder's account: the behaviour
numbering is aligned with the reds contract, and all six reds are provably
the output of a broken implementation. The rule meets every behaviour in
the brief and its acceptance line holds through `bisellium check`. The one
open item is a stale certificate created by the cleanup commit's file
ordering, fixed by one command and recorded above.

